# api-service agent — learnings log

## 2026-08-24 - Jute Mechanical & Maintenance Service Layer

- **Route naming conflicts:** none. `MECH_` prefix was unused across all of `src/` before
  this run (verified by grep). One near-collision worth knowing: `/api/mechMaster`
  (machine master, `src/masters/mechineMaster.py`) already exists and is UNRELATED to
  `/api/mechMaintMasters`. Also `MECH_PM_SCHEDULE_*` (checklist TEMPLATE master, router
  `/api/mechMaintMasters`) vs `MECH_PM_*` (PM EXECUTIONS, router `/api/mechPM`) — two
  different routers, near-identical key prefixes. Commented inline so nobody binds the
  wrong one.
- **Mapper edge cases:** no mapper file was needed — the backend already returns
  display-name columns from its SQL (`dept_desc AS dept_name`, `machine_type_name`,
  `component_name`), so the FE consumes rows directly. Null handling is in the TYPES
  instead: every LEFT-JOINed display name is `string | null`, and `machine_attr_id` +
  every attribute column on `MechMachineAttr` are nullable because that table endpoint
  LEFT JOINs *from* `machine_mst` (machines with no attr row still appear).
- **Shared utilities reused:** `fetchWithCookie` only. There is no shared `mapToOptions`
  helper in `src/utils/` — each service defines its own local helpers. Followed
  `inventoryReportService.ts` (camelCase param objects + `URLSearchParams`) and
  `accountingService.ts` (`${ROUTE}/${id}` path-param append, `if (error) throw`).
- **Backend response quirks:**
  - `{ data, total }` on list/report endpoints; bare `{ data }` on setup/by-id/write.
    Wrote `requestList` to tolerate a missing `total` (falls back to `data.length`).
  - `machine_mst` has NO `co_id` and NO `branch_id`. `branch_id` everywhere is DERIVED
    via `machine_mst -> dept_mst.branch_id` and is therefore nullable in every row type.
  - `spell_mst` keys activity by `status = 1`, not `active = 1`.
  - `dept_mst` and `sub_dept_mst` have NO `active` column at all.
  - No approval workflow on this module — `status` is OPEN/UNDER_REPAIR/CLOSED (a repair
    state), NOT the 21/1/20/3/4/6 document lifecycle. Do not wire ApprovalActionsBar.
- **[FIELD-MAPPING]** backend -> UI, non-obvious ones:
  - `dept_mst.dept_desc` -> `dept_name` (aliased in SQL, so the FE row key is `dept_name`)
  - `sub_dept_mst.sub_dept_desc` -> `sub_dept_name`
  - `machine_type_mst.machine_type_name` == the Excel workbook's "section"
  - `mech_code` is the machine's short code on `machine_mst` (not a module prefix)
  - PM detail rows snapshot `task_desc` from `jute_mech_pm_schedule` at execution time —
    `pm_schedule_id` may be null for an ad-hoc task.
- **[API-QUIRK]** Report paths on `/api/mechMaintReports` are KEBAB-CASE
  (`/breakdown-register`, `/top-problem-machines`) while every other router in this module
  is snake_case (`/entry_create`, `/pm_schedule_table`). That is the house reports
  convention — do not "normalize" it.
- **[API-QUIRK]** `date-machine-grid` does not return a plain row list: it returns
  `{ data: [{tran_date, "<machine_id>": "RF/DRJ", ...}], columns: [{machine_id, machine_name}] }`
  with DYNAMIC machine-id keys. Typed as `{ tran_date: string } & Record<string, unknown>`
  and given its own non-generic fetch function.
- **Assumptions to verify once the backend routers land** (`masters.py`,
  `breakdown_entry.py`, `overhaul.py`, `pm.py`, `reports.py` did not exist at this run —
  only `constants.py`, `models.py`, `masters_query.py` did):
  1. Enum dropdowns (`disciplines`, `downtime_types`, `criticalities`, `pm_frequencies`,
     `pm_results`, `overhaul_types`, `done_by_options`, `statuses`) are typed as
     `{ value, label }[]`, the natural serialization of the `*_LABELS` dicts in
     `constants.py`. If the backend emits bare string arrays instead, `MechEnumOption`
     must change.
  2. `top-problem-machines` is typed as one flat row list with a `rank_type`
     (DOWNTIME|COUNT|REPEAT) discriminator, to fit the spec's uniform `{data, total}`
     contract. If it instead returns three named arrays, retype it.
- **What should change:** when the backend author writes `masters.py`, cross-check the two
  assumptions above FIRST — they are the only guesses in the file. Also: this repo has no
  shared `Option`/`mapToOptions` utility despite ~24 service files each rolling their own
  dropdown shapes; extracting `src/utils/optionMappers.ts` would pay for itself.
